==============================================================================
S1. TYPING TABLE probes
==============================================================================

--- S1.1  Squarefree divisors of N=p^2 q r : F_2^3, PG(2,2), the Fano plane, and the octonion orientation ---
N = 60 = 2^2*3*5 : F=10 S=7 U=3 ; F=S+U: True
   shape p      N=     7 : F= 0 S=0 U=0
   shape p^3    N=   343 : F= 2 S=1 U=1
   shape p^2qr  N=  7007 : F=10 S=7 U=3
   the user's {0,2,10} = F at the three DB1 shapes p, p^3, p^2qr (inherited arithmetic_braids_20260917_divisors.md DB1).
divisor box [0,2]x[0,1]^2 has 12 points = 8 (Boolean cube {squarefree divisors incl. 1}) + 3 (nonsquarefree proper: [4, 12, 20]) + 1 (N)
PROVED (+checked 64 products): squarefree divisors under (multiply, drop squares) = F_2^3 ; 1 = zero vector.
Fano lines of the divisor lattice (triples of proper squarefree divisors with square product):
   [2, 3, 6]      product 36 = 6^2
   [2, 5, 10]     product 100 = 10^2
   [2, 15, 30]    product 900 = 30^2
   [3, 5, 15]     product 225 = 15^2
   [3, 10, 30]    product 900 = 30^2
   [5, 6, 30]     product 900 = 30^2
   [6, 10, 15]    product 900 = 30^2
PROVED: 7 points, 7 lines, 3 points/line, 3 lines/point = PG(2,2).
orientations of the 7 Fano lines: 128 ; giving an ALTERNATIVE algebra (linearized alternativity on the basis): 16
checked: the valid orientations satisfy |xy|^2=|x|^2|y|^2 exactly on 120 random integer pairs (composition algebra = octonions).
hostile witness: orientation (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) fails linearized alternativity at basis triple (1, 2, 4)
first valid orientation (line -> cyclic order): [((2, 4, 6), 'a->b->c'), ((1, 4, 5), 'a->b->c'), ((3, 4, 7), 'a->b->c'), ((1, 2, 3), 'a->b->c'), ((2, 5, 7), 'a->b->c'), ((1, 6, 7), 'a->c->b'), ((3, 5, 6), 'a->c->b')]
VERDICT: the divisor lattice of p^2qr fixes the 7 points (S=7) and the 7 lines (square-product triples) = F_2^3 / PG(2,2),
         but nothing in the lattice selects one of the 16 octonion orientations out of 128: the multiplication is LOST DATA.
         16 = 128/8 is rank-nullity of the incidence map (inherited arithmetic_braids2_20260917_fano_code.md sec.1); re-derived here
         by brute alternativity as an independent path.  Fano points 7 = S(p^2qr), dim 3 = U(p^2qr).

--- S1.2  Cayley-Dickson dimensions vs Omega / omega ---
   a=2        Omega= 1 omega=1 d(N)=  2 2^omega=2  2^Omega=2  divisor box = 2 points
   b=6        Omega= 2 omega=2 d(N)=  4 2^omega=4  2^Omega=4  divisor box = 4 points
   a^2b=24    Omega= 4 omega=2 d(N)=  8 2^omega=4  2^Omega=16  divisor box = 8 points
   c^2b=5400  Omega= 8 omega=3 d(N)= 48 2^omega=8  2^Omega=256  divisor box = 48 points
PROVED: Omega is additive (Omega(MN)=Omega(M)+Omega(N)), Cayley-Dickson dimension is multiplicative (dim doubles),
        so 'Omega(a^2 b)=4 <-> dim H = 4' and 'Omega(c^2 b)=8 <-> dim O = 8' compare 2*Omega(a)+Omega(b) = 2+2, 6+2 with 2^2, 2^3:
        the equalities 4=4 and 8=8 are two coincidences of small numbers; the next member 'Omega(d^2 b) = 10' (d a 4-almost-prime)
        has no Cayley-Dickson partner (dimension 16 = sedenions).  Inherited DB3: literal C^2B is the 8-almost-primes, not p^2qr.
        d(N) = prod(a_i+1) <= 2^Omega(N) with equality iff N squarefree; and d(N) = 2^omega(N) iff N squarefree.
        (checked 2<=N<=2000).  The only functorial match: for SQUAREFREE N with omega(N)=j the squarefree-divisor group is F_2^j,
        its real group algebra R[F_2^j] has dimension 2^j = d(N), and the Cayley-Dickson algebra of that dimension is a
        twisted group algebra R_F[F_2^j] (CITED: Albuquerque-Majid, 'Quasialgebra structure of the octonions', J. Algebra 220 (1999)).
        Map: N=1->R, p->C, pq->H, pqr->O (omega = 0,1,2,3).  LOST: the cocycle F (signs/orientation).  a^2b and c^2b are NOT squarefree,
        their divisor boxes (8 and 48 points above) are not groups: VERDICT numerology for the Omega version.

--- S1.3  Bott periodicity 8: probe ---
odd moduli m<2000 with ord_m(2)=8 exactly: [17, 51, 85, 255]  (all divide 2^8-1=255)
Collatz row moduli and their orders of 2:  [(3, 2), (9, 6), (7, 3), (63, 6), (21, 6), (27, 18)]
R-braid period on rows = ord_9(4) = 3; source period mod 42 and target mod 63: 3 (inherited row_braid_typing RB4).
VERDICT: NO MAP FOUND.  8 = Bott period is the Morita period of the Clifford cocycle on F_2^n (Cl_{n+8} = Cl_n (x) M_16(R));
         the shared object with S1.1 is only 'twisted group algebra of F_2^n' (Clifford AND octonion are such: CITED Albuquerque-Majid).
         No Collatz modulus has ord(2)=8; the row/braid periods are 2,3,6.  Nothing in the divisor lattice carries a cocycle.
         The one '8' with a mechanism is 2(r+1)=2^r iff r=3 (self-dual RM(1,3)): inherited fano_code.md sec.5, not Bott.

--- S1.4  6/pi^2 candidates: exact densities at 10^6 odd n ---
(a) odd n with T(n) squarefree             : 911902/1000000 = 0.911902 ; predicted prod_{p>=5}(1-1/p^2) = 0.911891 = 9/pi^2 = 0.911891 = (3/2)*6/pi^2
(b) odd n with gcd(n,T(n))=1               : 1000000/1000000 = 1 exactly (PROVED: gcd(n,3n+1)=1); NOT 6/pi^2 (hostile control)
    odd n with gcd(n+2,T(n))=1             : 0.800000 ; PROVED: gcd(n+2,3n+1) | 5, so density = 1 - 1/5 = 0.800000
(c) odd n with n AND T(n) squarefree        : 737515/1000000 = 0.737515 ; predicted (8/9)*prod_{p>=5}(1-2/p^2) = 0.737449 (Mirsky-type product, NOT a multiple of 6/pi^2)
    (a) restricted to row n = 1 mod 6: 0.911887  (predicted 9/pi^2 = 0.911891, row-uniform)
    (a) restricted to row n = 3 mod 6: 0.911917  (predicted 9/pi^2 = 0.911891, row-uniform)
    (a) restricted to row n = 5 mod 6: 0.911902  (predicted 9/pi^2 = 0.911891, row-uniform)
WHY (PROVED, CRT + Moebius): T(n) squarefree iff p^2 !| 3n+1 for every odd p; for p>=5 that is one excluded class of n mod p^2,
     for p=3 it is automatic (3n+1 = 1 mod 3), for p=2 the odd part is taken.  Density = prod_{p>=5}(1-1/p^2) = (6/pi^2)*(4/3)*(9/8) = 9/pi^2.
     The factor 3/2 over 6/pi^2 is exactly the two local factors killed by 'odd core' (p=2) and 'image is 2 mod 3' (p=3).
     Coprimality of (n, T(n)) or of any two affine forms in n is trivial: gcd(an+b, cn+d) | (ad-bc).  So 6/pi^2 = P(gcd=1) cannot arise
     from Collatz-linear pairs; it arises only through squarefreeness, and there with the local factors at 2 and 3 removed.
     Negative n: T(-n) = -T_{-1}(n) (inherited summand.md sec.7) swaps rows 1 and 5 and preserves |.|-squarefreeness: the same densities.

