==============================================================================
S3. Six bold conjectures, probes, follow-ups
==============================================================================

--- C1 (G-map x primes): the greedy 3-adic stopping-time law on primes equals the law on composites ---
  pooled m<=10^6, 3!|m: primes 78497 (mean steps 28.7373, max 73) ; composites 588169 (mean 29.0404, max 74) ; TV distance 0.0174
  CORRECTED NEAR MISS (recovered draft): its hostile control 'm=1 mod 9 vs m=8 mod 9 differ' is FALSE:
    TV(1 mod 9, 8 mod 9) = 0.0072 pooled, 0.0088 in [2^19,2^20); the two classes have equal means (31.616 vs 31.630 in the block).
  TRUE PICTURE: the step count grows like log m, so any pooled comparison mixes sizes; primes are relatively denser at small m,
    which produces the pooled mean gap 0.303.  Size-controlled comparison (dyadic blocks):
    [131072,262144): primes  10749 mean 27.4540 ; composites  76632 mean 27.4152 ; TV 0.0187 ; |mean gap| 0.0388
    [262144,524288): primes  20390 mean 29.0968 ; composites 154373 mean 29.1176 ; TV 0.0143 ; |mean gap| 0.0208
    [524288,1048576): primes  35108 mean 30.6987 ; composites 282034 mean 30.7757 ; TV 0.0122 ; |mean gap| 0.0770
  hostile controls (both DIFFER): block [2^17,2^18) vs [2^19,2^20): TV 0.2085 (size); classes 4 vs 5 mod 9 in [2^19,2^20): TV 0.2953
    (class means in [2^19,2^20) by m mod 9: [(1, 31.616), (2, 29.898), (4, 28.188), (5, 33.345), (7, 29.927), (8, 31.63)] )
  mechanism: the first J greedy letters are a function of m mod 3^(J+1) (inherited extended_collatz_scc Theorem 4.1), so the
    residue class of m matters (4 mod 9: first step x1/3; 5 mod 9: x8/3) and primes are equidistributed in the unit classes (Dirichlet):
    primes vs composites mod 81: TV 0.0040.
  STATUS: SURVIVES in the size-controlled form (FINITE-EXACT at 10^6: TV < 0.03 in each block); PROVED for every fixed-depth prefix
    event (Theorem 4.1 + Dirichlet); the full-law equality is HEURISTIC.  The pooled statement is REFUTED as stated (size mixing).
  follow-up F1: within a fixed block AND a fixed class mod 9, do primes and composites still agree? -> [(1, 31.502, 31.63, 0.0272), (2, 29.795, 29.911, 0.0317), (4, 28.108, 28.198, 0.0268), (5, 33.29, 33.351, 0.029), (7, 29.882, 29.933, 0.0288), (8, 31.632, 31.63, 0.0234)]  (all TV < 0.04 with ~5800 primes per cell: yes)

--- C2 (signed cycles x cycle gate): b = 2^K - 3^L  =>  EVERY composition of K into L parts is realized as a cycle word ---
  INHERITED (PROVED in 04-computation/experiments/collatz_mod6_20260917_three_n_plus_k_catalan.py, necklace theorem; and the
  q | b iff of arithmetic_braids2_20260917_signed_cycles.md sec.3): 3B(w)+2^K-3^L = 2^(k_1) B(rot w), so for b=2^K-3^L the orbit of B(w)
  realizes w and closes; distinct necklaces give distinct cycles.  Re-verified here as an independent path (bold form: ALL compositions).
  representations b = 2^K - 3^L with |b|<=99, K<70, L<45 (FINITE-EXACT): {-79: [(1, 4)], -77: [(2, 4)], -73: [(3, 4)], -65: [(4, 4)], -49: [(5, 4)], -25: [(1, 3)], -23: [(2, 3)], -19: [(3, 3)], -17: [(6, 4)], -11: [(4, 3)], -7: [(1, 2)], -5: [(2, 2)], -1: [(1, 1), (3, 2)], 1: [(2, 1)], 5: [(3, 1), (5, 3)], 7: [(4, 2)], 13: [(4, 1), (8, 5)], 23: [(5, 2)], 29: [(5, 1)], 37: [(6, 3)], 47: [(7, 4)], 55: [(6, 2)], 61: [(6, 1)]}
   b= 1 = 2^2-3^1 : 1 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 1 = rotation classes 1
   b= 5 = 2^3-3^1 : 1 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 1 = rotation classes 1
   b= 5 = 2^5-3^3 : 6 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 2 = rotation classes 2
   b= 7 = 2^4-3^2 : 3 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 2 = rotation classes 2
   b=13 = 2^4-3^1 : 1 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 1 = rotation classes 1
   b=13 = 2^8-3^5 : 35 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 7 = rotation classes 7
   b=23 = 2^5-3^2 : 4 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 2 = rotation classes 2
   b=29 = 2^5-3^1 : 1 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 1 = rotation classes 1
   b=37 = 2^6-3^3 : 10 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 4 = rotation classes 4
   b=47 = 2^7-3^4 : 20 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 5 = rotation classes 5
   b=55 = 2^6-3^2 : 5 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 3 = rotation classes 3
   b=61 = 2^6-3^1 : 1 compositions, all realized; distinct census cycles they populate: 1 = rotation classes 1
  bold sub-conjecture 'c(b) is maximal among b<=99 exactly at the doubly-representable b (5, 13)': max c(b) = 19 at b=[65] -> REFUTED (witness b=[65], c=19: 65 = 5*13 inherits both scaled families)
  follow-up F2: fixed points of 3n+b are exactly n = b/(2^K-3), K>=1, so #1-cycles = #{K>=1 : (2^K-3) | b}: check -> PASS for all b<=99 (PROVED + FINITE-EXACT).  b=65=5*13 has 4 fixed points: [(5,), (13,), (65,), (-65,)]

--- C3 (floor sums x rows/braid): the squarefree detector Z_3 on the trunk: 'N_j squarefree iff gcd(j, N_j) = gcd(j, 3)' ---
  INHERITED (PROVED, arithmetic_braids2_20260917_floor_reciprocity.md; collatz_mod6_20260917_floor_sums_odd_functions.md S1-S2):
    sum_{k<n} floor(k^3/n) = (n-2)(n-1)(n+1)/4 + Z_3(n)/2 with Z_3(n) = n/n_3 - 1, so the cube floor identity holds iff n is squarefree.
    re-verified on the trunk terms N_2..N_10 (Z_3 by brute force for j<=6).  Detector reading: identity holds at N_j iff N_j squarefree.
  PROVED exact law (LTE): for an odd prime p, v_p(4^j-1) = v_p(4^d-1) + v_p(j/d) when d = ord_p(4) | j (else 0); and v_p(4^d-1) = v_p(2^{p-1}-1).
    Hence p^2 | N_j (p>=5) iff ord_p(4) | j and (p | j or p is a Wieferich prime); 9 | N_j iff 9 | j (v_3(N_j) = v_3(j)).
    So the conjecture 'N_j squarefree iff gcd(j,N_j) = gcd(j,3)' is exactly the statement that no Wieferich prime interferes.
  FINITE-EXACT j<=40: conjecture holds; non-squarefree trunk terms and their square factors: [(9, {3: 2}), (10, {5: 2}), (18, {3: 2}), (20, {5: 2}), (21, {7: 2}), (27, {3: 3}), (30, {5: 2}), (36, {3: 2}), (40, {5: 2})]
  REFUTED in general: Wieferich primes 1093, 3511 (CITED: the only ones below 6.7e15, Dorais-Klyve 2011 / PrimeGrid) with ord_1093(4) = 182, ord_3511(4) = 1755:
    witness j=182 = 2*7*13: 1093^2 | N_182 while 1093 !| 182; gcd(182, N_182) = 1 = gcd(182,3) (ord_7(4)=3 and ord_13(4)=6 do not divide 182,
      so the p | j mechanism is silent), i.e. the conjecture predicts 'squarefree' and N_182 is not: REFUTED, Wieferich witness.
    minimal witness: j=182 is the least j with a Wieferich contribution, conditional on no unknown Wieferich prime p with ord_p(4) < 182;
      unconditionally the conjecture is FINITE-EXACT-true for j<=40 and false at j=182.
  STATUS: PROVED law; conjecture FINITE-EXACT for j<=40 and REFUTED at j=182 (Wieferich).  The floor-sum squarefree detector on
    the Collatz trunk sees the Wieferich primes: cross-thread map (rows/braid trunk) x (floor sums) with a concrete sidecar (LTE).
  follow-up F3: is N_j EVER squarefree for j = 0 mod 9? No (9 | N_j).  Fraction of j<=40 with N_j squarefree: 31/40.

--- C4 (sandwich x squares): N_PS - N_SP = #{p>=5: p^2<=6K+1, p^2-2 prime} + bounded residual ? ---
  K=100000: N_PS-N_SP = 41 (matches collatz_mod6_20260917_sandwich_bias.md sec.1) ; square-driven pairs (p^2-2 prime, p^2) = 46 ; residual after squares = -5 ; independence pred = 36.6
  K=1000000: N_PS-N_SP = 412 (matches collatz_mod6_20260917_sandwich_bias.md sec.1) ; square-driven pairs (p^2-2 prime, p^2) = 99 ; residual after squares = 313 ; independence pred = 113.2
  CORRECTED NEAR MISS (recovered draft): its status line quoted square counts 64 and 137; the true counts of pairs (6k-1, 6k+1) = (p^2-2, p^2)
    with both entries as stated are 46 and 99 (two independent counts agree).
  STATUS: REFUTED as 'bounded residual': residual after removing squares is -5 at 10^5 and 313 at 10^6 (grows with the drift, not bounded).
  The square term is PROVED to be one-sided (p^2 = 1 mod 6 always sits on the right), but it is small against the mixed-class semiprime bias.
  follow-up F4: does the p^2 count alone explain the S-column excess of the marginals?  S_1 - S_5 identity (inherited) already has pi'(sqrt x)/2:
    K=100000: pi'(sqrt x) = 135, (S_1 - S_5) = 66 (col-row sum of class 2) ; half the squares = 67.5
    K=1000000: pi'(sqrt x) = 361, (S_1 - S_5) = 244 (col-row sum of class 2) ; half the squares = 180.5

--- C5 (3n+b x G-map conjugacy): T_b(b n) = b T_1(n), G_b(b m) = b G_1(m); c(b) >= 4 and superadditivity ---
  PROVED: (3(bn)+b)/2^v = b(3n+1)/2^v ; so b*C_1 subset C_b, c(b) >= 4 for every b coprime to 6 (checked b<=99).
  (This is the dilation half of the content theorem, arithmetic_braids2_20260917_signed_cycles.md sec.2: cycles of T_b = disjoint union over g | b of g*(primitive cycles of T_{b/g}).)
  b<=99 with c(b) = 4 exactly (no cycle beyond the four scaled Collatz cycles): [1]
  superadditivity for coprime b1,b2: c(b1 b2) >= c(b1) + c(b2) - 4 (scaled cycles b2*C_{b1}, b1*C_{b2} meet only in b1b2*C_1):
    violations among b1*b2<=99: [] -> PROVED inequality holds (FINITE-EXACT confirmation)
  PROVED (+checked m<3000, b=5,7,11,13): G_b(b m) = b G_1(m) with the same letter; the residue chain mod 9 of G_b is the
    chain of G_1 conjugated by multiplication by b^{-1} mod 9, so the stationary law, E[letter]=1 and the drift log(2/3)
    (inherited extended_collatz_scc S4.2) are b-independent.  What changes with b is the CYCLE set (C2), not the local law.
  follow-up F5: are the 'new' cycles (beyond b*C_1) of 3n+b all divisor-gate cycles n = bB/(2^K-3^L) with (2^K-3^L) !| B? -> 175 new cycles, 175 have (2^K-3^L) !| B (need the factor b), 0 have (2^K-3^L) | B

--- C6 (Cipolla trunk x primes): 'N_j is a base-2 pseudoprime iff j is a prime >= 5' ---
  FINITE-EXACT j<=40: holds (S2.3 table).  REFUTED at j=85 (= N_4 = 5*17): N_85 is a base-2 pseudoprime with composite index;
    mechanism: for odd j with 3 !| j, 6j | 4^j-4 iff 4^{j-1} = 1 mod j, i.e. j is a base-4 Fermat (pseudo)prime; 85, 91, 341, ... are base-4 pseudoprimes.
    hostile: 15 is a base-4 pseudoprime but 3 | 15, so N_15 is NOT a pseudoprime (9 !| 4^15-4 since 3 !| 14): the factor 3 of the trunk matters.
  follow-up F6 (recursion, PROVED in S2.3): W is closed under j -> N_j, so the index set contains p, N_p, N_{N_p}, ... for every prime p>=5;
    the first composite index NOT of the form N_j is 91 = 7*13 (85 = N_4 is of that form; 341 = N_5 and 5461 = N_7 are the tower's second level).

==============================================================================
S4. 3N-5 = 3(N-2)+1: exact relation to Collatz under the shift n -> n-2
==============================================================================
PROVED (+checked odd |n|<=10^5): T_{-5}(n) = T(n-2) exactly (3n-5 = 3(n-2)+1, same 2-adic valuation).
  Hence with the shift s(m)=m+2: s^{-1} T_{-5} s (m) = T(m) - 2.  This is NOT a conjugacy of T with itself: the exact
  discrepancy is the constant -2 after every odd step, i.e. T_{-5} is conjugate to the map m -> T(m)-2, and the orbit of n
  under 3n-5 is the orbit of n-2 under 'Collatz then subtract 2 each odd step'.
  rows mod 6 -> single-halving image (3n-5)/2 :
    n = 6j+1 : (3n-5)/2 = 9*j - 1  = 8 mod 9  (Collatz row image of 6j+1 would be 2 mod 9)
    n = 6j+3 : (3n-5)/2 = 9*j + 2  = 2 mod 9  (Collatz row image of 6j+3 would be 5 mod 9)
    n = 6j+5 : (3n-5)/2 = 9*j + 5  = 5 mod 9  (Collatz row image of 6j+5 would be 8 mod 9)
  PROVED: the 3n-5 row map is the Collatz row map precomposed with r -> r-2 mod 6 (1->5->3->1): images 8,2,5 instead of 2,5,8;
  all images are 2 mod 3 exactly as for Collatz (multiples of 3 are leaves for 3n-5 as well).
  cycles of 3n-5 on odd integers, both signs, min-element |n|<=2*10^5 (= negatives of the 9 cycles of 3n+5):
    length 17  min |n|    347 : (-347, -523, -787, -1183, -1777, -667, -1003, -1507, '...')
    length 17  min |n|    187 : (-187, -283, -427, -643, -967, -1453, -1091, -1639, '...')
    length  3  min |n|     23 : (-23, -37, -29)
    length  3  min |n|     19 : (-19, -31, -49)
    length  1  min |n|      5 : (-5,)
    length  1  min |n|      1 : (-1,)
    length  1  min |n|      5 : (5,)
    length  2  min |n|     25 : (25, 35)
    length  7  min |n|     85 : (85, 125, 185, 275, 205, 305, 455)
  POSITIVE 3n-5 cycles = 3 (the user's '3N-5 on positives' = 3N+5 on negatives): [(5,), (25, 35), (85, 125, 185, 275, 205, 305, 455)]
  the two 3-cycles live on the POSITIVE side of 3n+5 = NEGATIVE side of 3n-5: (19,31,49),(23,37,29),
  and are the C2 construction for 5 = 2^5-3^3 (words (1,1,3) and (1,2,2)); 3n-5 has them as (-19,-31,-49),(-23,-37,-29).
  shifted picture: a 3n-5 cycle (n_i) is a cycle (n_i - 2) of m -> T(m)-2, e.g. fixed point -5 of 3n-5 <-> m=-7: T(-7)-2 = -5-2 = -7.
  PROVED: 3n-5 on negatives = -(3n+5 on positives); 3n-5 on positives = -(3n+5 on negatives) (sign conjugation, inherited summand note sec.7;
  the nine cycles, their contents d in {1,5} and minimal parameters q in {1,5}: arithmetic_braids2_20260917_signed_cycles.md sec.5).

